# Linux Storage Management (LVM)

### Initial Configuration

The Ubuntu Server VM was provisioned with a 40 GiB virtual disk. However, the default guided LVM installation allocated only 19 GiB to the root logical volume.

### Investigation

The following commands were used to inspect disk and LVM allocation:

```bash
lsblk
df -h
sudo pvs
sudo vgs
sudo lvs
```

The investigation identified 19 GiB of unallocated space within the `ubuntu-vg` volume group.

### Initial Storage Allocation

| Component | Size |
|---|---:|
| Virtual Disk | 40 GiB |
| Boot Partition | 2 GiB |
| LVM Volume Group | 38 GiB |
| Root Logical Volume | 19 GiB |
| Unallocated LVM Space | 19 GiB |

### Resolution

Extended the root logical volume and resized the filesystem using the following command:

```bash
sudo lvextend -l +100%FREE -r /dev/ubuntu-vg/ubuntu-lv
```

### Verification

The following commands are used to verify the logical volume expansion and filesystem capacity:

```bash
df -h /
sudo vgs
sudo lvs
```

### Skills Demonstrated

- Linux filesystem management
- Logical Volume Manager (LVM)
- Storage capacity analysis
- Logical volume expansion
- Online filesystem resizing
- Command-line administration

### Lessons Learned

- Virtual disk capacity and usable filesystem capacity are not necessarily the same.
- LVM allows flexible storage allocation and expansion.
- Storage capacity should be verified before deploying applications.
- Logical volumes can be expanded without reinstalling the operating system.
